A||||9||Adrift||Holes in floor under carpet||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||09-20-2001||11:49 PM||||I pulled up my carpet to dye them only to find the insalation underthem was waterlogged - even though the car has been sitting in my garage ever since purchase 4 weeks ago! So i naturally pulled away the waterlogged cloth and dried off the floor only to find that rust had start to form. <P>"Not a big deal" I thought, not seeing any holes to the ground. I then noticed patches of black electrical tape on the floor boards on both the passenger and driver's side. Lifting the electrical tape revealed hevality rusted metal used to cover holes, about 3 x 3 inchs in width, in my floor! There are about four of these holes in total. <P>Is this a common fiero problem? Does this cause some structural itegrity problems? I don't know how to weld - with this in mind, How do I fix it? Can I use fibreglass? Is this a bad idea?<P>Thanks,<BR>Ari<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||BlackDragon||09-20-2001||11:58 PM||||use some kind of rust remover. i suggest naval jelly. then paint it to prevent future rust. then drill holes and bolt a peice of metal there (aluminum)<P>------------------<BR>We are all different for a reason<P><BR>BlackDragon||||reg||1
Z||000002||LarryB||09-21-2001||05:16 AM||||you can also pop-rivet the metal there as well, did this years ago with a Mercury Bobcat (upscale Ford Pinto).  I cut out the punky metal remaining and riveted in a new piece of metal cut from a recycled road sign, putting it in from the inside of the car. After you put the new sheet of metal in, get under the car (do it the right way, don't just use a jack to raise it and slide underneath!) and use a can of the expanding foam insulation to run a bead on the inside of the seam between the old and new metal.  That'll make sure you have a good seal so water and exhaust gasses can't get inside. After it cured I used a can of undercoating and sprayed the area from underneath as well but that's up to you whether you want to do that.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroBUZZ||09-21-2001||06:45 AM||||There are 'plugs' put in at the factory and it is pretty common for them to be rusted out. Especially true on our Canadian cars. I don't think they serve any useful purpose. Unless you're doing a show restoration, patching them is fine. a piece of metal or f'glass will work.<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000004||jelly2m8||09-21-2001||09:57 AM||||The drain hole covers are still availible from the dealer. All 6 ( 3 per side)only run about 30 bux canuk.<P> Heres the part numbers. The big rear one is 10215806 There is one of these on each side.<P>The smaller front ones are 20579955 There are 2 of these on each side.<P> I keep 3 or 4 sets on hand at all times, as someone stated previously, they tend to rust out in the salt ban.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||09-21-2001||10:38 AM||||after you put a metal patch over it, be sure you spray the underside thick with rubberized undercoating. Its in parts stores for $4 or $5 a can. also spray inside around the patch. when its dry, Id spray the whole floor area with any color Rustoleum. You can also get brush on if you dont want to spray inside.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||09-21-2001||11:26 AM||||Those caps are really still available from Pontiac???  My Formula has one rogue cap that has decided to rust away while all the others are still good as new!  Since the car doesn't see winter salt, I have no idea why the one is bad but have been trying to decide what I should do with it.  Looks like I'll be making a trip to the pontiac dealer soon!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2m6siga.JPG">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-21-2001||08:13 PM||||The fix we used in upstate NY.... This may be long but it works and you won't have to do this twice.<P>clean away all rust on both sides of floor with a body grinder. (heavy duty sander will work.) <P>cut and clean patch. patch should over lap solid metal at least 1 inch (25mm).<P>All metal should be cleaned with alcohol or brake parts cleaner to remove all oil.<P>Obtain a can of roofing mastic from home/lumber supply store. Mastic is not the same as tar. It's an adhesive and never quite hardens. It will stick to nearly anything permanently.<P>Paint the top side of the floor pan with the mastic. <P>Paint the floor side of the patch with more mastic. (Odd shape patches have a top and bottom...)<P>Press the two parts firmly together. wiggle them ass you press down. Weight/hold the patch in patch in place.<P>drill one hole thru both parts and rivit them. repeat on opposite side of patch. this keeps it from moving.<P>put in another rivit avery 1-2 inches depending on size/location of patch. Floor patches don't need many rivits. The mastic won't let go onces it sets.<P>coat the top of the patch area completely with mastic and put a peice of plastic or Kraft paper over it to keep it from bonding to the carpet. (Kraft is what brown paper bags are made from.)<P>get under the car and coat the bottom with mastic. make sure you work it in around the rivits good.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Adrift||09-22-2001||08:44 PM||||I just filled in the holes, I first laid fibreglass over the holes (To waterproof them), then put sheetmetal on top of them, using short bolt/screw things to hold it down. Going to tar them or whatever tomorrow. Pictures arriving soon.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierohoho||09-25-2001||10:41 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B>The drain hole covers are still availible from the dealer. All 6 ( 3 per side)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the part numbers.<P>I've been trying to get someone down south to pull me a set as mine rusted out too, didn't have any luck though. <P>I ordered them Monday and sure confused the parts guy. <P>He tried to get the Fiero #'s first then said only one was available but I had him order the #'s you gave which shows both types in stock.<P>I would guess that the Fiero isn't the only car these were used on.<P>Should have em by Thursday.<P>Where did you come up with the part numbers anyway?<p>[This message has been edited by fierohoho (edited 09-25-2001).]||||reg||1
